1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,885
Type a digit from zero to nine, and the green light changes brightness. A hardware

2
00:00:05,885 --> 00:00:09,300
timer does the fast blinking. Let's read every line.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:18,666
In the P1 folder, open demo code, then 02 rgb pwm reference. We need the lesson file

4
00:00:18,666 --> 00:00:20,060
and pin_mux.c.

5
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:25,947
Your project needs the tpm driver. Paste the lesson over your main file, and the new

6
00:00:25,947 --> 00:00:28,640
pin file into the board folder. Save both.

7
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,100
The new pin file connects the green light to the timer.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:37,180
Line 83 gives us print f and get char, for talking through the terminal.

9
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:42,320
Line 84 knows this board. It also brings in the clock and pin tools it needs.

10
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:49,540
Line 85 is new: the TPM driver. TPM means timer and PWM module.

11
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:57,560
Line 87 brings in the pin setup. Line 93 is where main, your program, begins.

12
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:05,143
Switch on lines 121 to 125 with the comment shortcut: Command slash, or Control

13
00:01:05,143 --> 00:01:06,560
slash on Windows.

14
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,860
Line 121 gives every pin its job.

15
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,680
Line 122 starts the chip's heartbeat.

16
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:18,620
Line 123 opens the text line to your computer.

17
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:26,352
Line 125 picks which clock feeds the timer. A timer can listen to different clocks,

18
00:01:26,352 --> 00:01:27,920
so we must choose.

19
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:36,287
Switch on line 147. It prints a title, with backslash r backslash n to start new

20
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:36,840
lines.

21
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:43,760
Switch on lines 184 to 194. This is an order form for the brightness signal.

22
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:48,660
Line 184 nicknames timer TPM zero.

23
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:55,960
Line 185 nicknames channel four. That output is wired to the green light.

24
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:00,300
Line 187 makes an empty form for the timer's settings.

25
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,720
Line 188 makes an empty form for one output signal.

26
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:11,380
Line 189 says low true: our light counts as on when the pin is low.

27
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:17,587
Line 190 remembers the starting duty cycle: ten percent. The U means a number that

28
00:02:17,587 --> 00:02:18,840
is never negative.

29
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:21,580
What does ten percent duty look like?

30
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:27,080
Your turn. Pause the video.

31
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:27,560
Dim.

32
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:33,840
Line 192 writes the channel number into the form. The words in brackets turn the

33
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:36,480
number into the type the driver expects.

34
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:40,660
Line 193 writes the level, low true.

35
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:47,640
Line 194 writes the duty cycle. A dot reaches one box inside the form.

36
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:52,240
Switch on lines 223 and 224.

37
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:58,431
Line 223 fills the timer form with sensible default settings. The and sign tells the

38
00:02:58,431 --> 00:03:00,260
function where the form is.

39
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:05,480
Line 224 gives those settings to the real timer. It still isn't counting.

40
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:10,000
Switch on lines 257 to 261.

41
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:16,100
Line 257 asks how fast the timer's clock ticks, and gives that a nickname.

42
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:23,401
Line 259 sets up the signal. In order: which timer, which signal form, how many

43
00:03:23,401 --> 00:03:24,520
signals, one.

44
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:33,152
Still on line 259: center aligned pulses, 24,000 of them each second, and the clock

45
00:03:33,152 --> 00:03:33,740
speed.

46
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:38,880
Line 261 starts the timer. Without it, nothing moves.

47
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:43,060
Save, build, and load using your project's debug setup.

48
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:49,800
You should see a soft green glow. In a bright room, shade the light with your hand.

49
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:56,813
Press Terminate. Then switch on lines 294 to 320. This is the part that listens to

50
00:03:56,813 --> 00:03:57,840
your keyboard.

51
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:01,540
Line 294 makes a box for the key you type.

52
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:05,240
Line 296, while one: repeat forever.

53
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:11,260
Line 298, do: run this block first, then check whether to repeat it.

54
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:15,840
Lines 300 to 302 ask you for a value.

55
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:21,830
Line 304 is the clever one. get char waits for one key. Your computer sends a

56
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:22,900
character code.

57
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:28,600
Subtracting hex 30, the code for the character zero, leaves the number itself.

58
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:31,340
If you type 7, what number is left?

59
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:36,840
Your turn. Pause the video.

60
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:37,400
Seven.

61
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:42,700
Lines 306 and 307 show what you typed.

62
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:49,226
Line 308 repeats the question while the value is greater than nine, which means you

63
00:04:49,226 --> 00:04:51,840
typed something that wasn't a digit.

64
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:58,040
Line 310 multiplies by ten. Five becomes fifty percent.

65
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:04,140
Line 312 briefly disconnects the channel from the pin, using zero.

66
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:09,440
Lines 314 and 315 write the new duty cycle into the timer.

67
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:15,780
Line 317 reconnects the channel with low true. Changing it this way avoids a glitch.

68
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:20,040
Line 319 confirms the update. Then the loop asks again.

69
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:29,292
Build, load and open your terminal. Click in it, then type 0, 5, and 9. The light

70
00:05:29,292 --> 00:05:32,960
should go dark, medium, then bright.

71
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:41,327
On line 259, change 24,000 to 50. Now the flashing may become visible. Skip this if

72
00:05:41,327 --> 00:05:46,120
flashing lights bother you. Restore 24,000 afterwards.

73
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:54,220
On line 189, change low true to high true. Type 1, then 9. Which is brighter now?

74
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:59,720
Your turn. Pause the video.

75
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:02,360
One, because on and off are swapped.

76
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:09,900
Restore low true. Great work: you can now explain every line of a PWM program.

